  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nAlthough widespread impactful weather may be limited across the \nnorthern Great Lakes much of this week, a still challenging forecast \nwith several impulses in fast west/northwest flow.  This results in \na myriad of model solutions and lower end precipitation chances \nthrough Thursday, largely dominated by mesoscale processes and \nnebulous forcing. A stronger upper shortwave trough slides toward \nthe Great Lakes this weekend, resulting in higher precipitation \nchances and cooler temperatures.  \n\nThe primary instability/moisture corridor remains well to the south \nof the area the next several days in proximity to a quasi- \nstationary frontal boundary draped across the northern Ohio \nValley. Hints of some remnant convection possibly trying to \nmove toward the area tonight out of Wisconsin. Low confidence in\nthis given waning instability and forcing but at least isolated\npotential. Areas of fog also possible again tonight. Wednesday \nfeatures weak flow and likely lake breeze dominated forcing for \nany convection. This places the best shower/storm chances closer\nto Lake Huron in northern lower and interior sections of \neastern upper. We may also see some scattered convection fire \nover southern parts of the area along a diffuse trough axis. \nFavorable shear profiles and some buoyancy, but once again the \nbest moisture/forcing remain well to our south. SPC has us in a\nmarginal severe risk during this timeframe. Additional low end \nshower/storm chances on Thursday, especially across southern \nparts of our area (south of M-32). \n\nReturn flow around departing high pressure for the weekend, \ncombined with a much stronger upper level shortwave trough and \nassociated surface low, will bring much better shower/storm\nchances to northern Michigan. Cooler and drier for early next \nweek behind this feature. Overall, temperatures will run near or\nslightly below normal for the weekend into the first part of \nnext week.",
